# Docs first

`docs/` (the MkDocs wiki) is the source of truth for how this platform works and
why. Consult it **before** designing, implementing, or answering questions:

| Question | Look in |
|---|---|
| Has this been decided already? | `docs/decisions.md` (numbered log — never re-litigate silently) |
| How does X work today? | `docs/architecture/` |
| Why is X shaped this way? What was rejected? | `docs/design/` |
| How do I run / deploy / test? | `docs/getting-started.md`, `docs/guides/` |

When answering a user, point them at the specific page (and section) that covers
their question, not just at the code. If the page is wrong or missing, fix it as
part of the change — a correct answer that leaves a stale doc behind is half done.

## Link hygiene (enforced)

`mkdocs build --strict` runs in the docs workflow before publish and **fails on
any broken link or missing anchor** (`validation: links: anchors: warn` in
`mkdocs.yml`). Rules that keep it green:

- Never markdown-link from docs to source files outside `docs/`
  (e.g. `[x](../../quant/api/main.py)`) — the built site cannot serve them.
  Write the path as inline code instead: `` `quant/api/main.py` ``.
- Anchor slugs collapse punctuation runs: a heading like
  `### 6.4 Service auth (implemented)` becomes `#64-service-auth-implemented`
  — em dashes and colons vanish and hyphen runs collapse to **one** hyphen.
  `#64-service-auth--implemented` (double hyphen) never matches anything.
- Every new page must be added to `nav:` in `mkdocs.yml`.
- After editing docs, run `mkdocs build --strict` locally and keep it at zero
  warnings.
